  "hypothesis": "For several exact Boolean queries sharing one revision-bound relation prefix, retaining the packed prefix once may avoid enough repeated prefix composition to justify a bounded regenerable physical view. If final TypedRelationBlock materialization dominates, the end-to-end benefit should remain weak and must not justify a cache service.",
  "finding": {
    "classification": "weak-mixed; does not justify a production cache",
    "summary": "Across six frozen synthetic cases with four query tails, the shared-prefix one-shot was only about 1-2 percent faster in five cases and about 1.3 percent slower in one case. Query-batch-only timing improved more, but final canonical materialization dominated the workload. The narrower retained-prefix idea is therefore evidence for avoiding a new cache/backend abstraction, not for promoting one.",
    "next_architectural_implication": "Keep the experiment contained. Investigate the final packed-support to canonical CSR/TypedRelationBlock materialization boundary before adding a cache service, another arithmetic backend, or GPU residency policy."
  },
